Evaluate member rules in morphism dependency order

Run local rule schedules within model graph components, propagating
results onward after each component closes. Order parent rules and
child traversals together so nested feedback closes at its common
owner. Keep feedback through ambient relations in global evaluation.

Give independent rule components separate schedules and repeat only
recursive components. Reuse stable input trees and inherited indices
within each rule loop to avoid reconstructing unchanged deltas.

Bind model owners before index selection. Keep consumption checkpoints
per stratum and owner while sharing snapshots and the latest secondary
indices across readers. Refresh owner checkpoints after model merges
and child graphs after transport. Preserve interrupted closure and
deferred allocation. Simultaneous scheduling remains the default and
selects one global stratum in the same evaluator.
Swap deferred work back on return so debug builds do not reserve a
full delta temporary for every stratum in the closure stack frame.

Store current relations and derive old/new views from each consumer's
checkpoint. Publish shared storage roots directly so equivalent
checkpoints keep sharing. Propagate current owned and inherited trees
without repartitioning them into global ages. Dynamic models also store
current facts; restoring one starts fresh consumer checkpoints.

Apply only each stratum's outputs and allocate only after immediate
closure. Skip relation canonicalization in rule steps that produce no
equality merges. Skip consumer views for unchanged owners and rules
blocked by empty inputs. Retain unrelated checkpoints when owners merge.
Generate conclusion buffers only for outputs used by rules.

Cover scope inference, long rule pipelines, nested propagation,
feedback, sparse updates, late graph changes, model merges,
single-stratum rounds, dynamic restoration, and interrupted evaluation
by executing generated evaluators across
native/desugared and naive/semi-naive configurations.
